# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Report content: chapters 1-5 of the Software Industry Gap Analysis report.

Block kinds understood by the builder:
  ('p', text)                      body paragraph (supports <b>/<i>)
  ('h2', text)                     second-level heading
  ('h3', text)                     third-level heading
  ('bullets', [items])             bulleted list
  ('stat_strip', [(big, label)])   3-up metric callout strip
  ('callout', big, label)          single metric callout box
  ('table', dict)                  dict(title, header, rows, ratios, align)
  ('chart', path, caption)         embedded chart image + caption
  ('quote', text)                  pull quote
"""

CH1 = {
    'num': '1', 'title': 'Executive Summary', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'This report asks a single question at the level of the whole industry: <b>what is the software industry missing?</b> Not "which product could be incrementally better," but which tools, connective links, and whole categories have never been built, or have been built so badly that they function as open wounds. The research deliberately prioritized gaps where nobody has done the work, or where everyone has done it badly, over crowded markets with strong incumbents. Everything that follows is filtered through that lens.'),
        ('p', 'Five parallel research tracks were run, each covering a different angle of the industry: developer tooling; integration, data, and system plumbing; unbuilt "missing links" between ecosystems; long-hated but never-fixed software categories; and frontier gaps spanning AI-era infrastructure and unsexy industries. Together they executed roughly sixty web searches, harvested evidence predominantly from 2024 through 2026, and discarded every candidate gap that turned out to be already served by a strong solution. The survivors are presented here with their evidence, their structural causes, and a realistic angle for a small team to ship them.'),
        ('stat_strip', [
            ('96%', 'of developers do not fully trust AI-generated code, yet only 48% always verify it (SonarSource, Jan 2026)'),
            ('$315K', 'average vendor lock-in cost per enterprise migration, with 62% citing data migration as the failure driver (Kong, Jun 2026)'),
            ('79%', 'of opportunity data that reps collect never makes it into the CRM (DevRev, 2026)'),
        ]),
        ('h2', 'Three headline conclusions'),
        ('bullets', [
            '<b>The industry has a translation problem, and AI just changed the economics of it.</b> Nearly every durable gap found — design to code, spec to test, docs to runtime, prose to formal properties — stayed unbuilt because translating between two representations cost more than the failures it prevented. That translation cost collapsed in 2025-2026, which is why so many decades-old gaps suddenly became buildable.',
            '<b>The deepest gaps are ownership vacuums, not technology vacuums.</b> Integrations break silently because nobody is paid to watch them; documentation rots because no test suite goes red; permission graphs drift because identity syncs accounts, not access. Connectivity exists almost everywhere — ownership does not.',
            '<b>Buyer-user mismatch is the one pattern AI does not fix.</b> In hated categories (CRM entry, expense reports, timesheets), the person who suffers is not the person who buys. The viable wedge is bottom-up: sell the suffering user a tool that removes their work, then sell the clean exhaust data to the buyer.',
        ]),
        ('p', 'The strongest immediately buildable opportunities for a two-to-four person team are: an <b>evals-in-CI adapter</b> that makes non-deterministic AI systems testable inside existing pipelines; a <b>verification and provenance ledger for AI-generated code</b>, sitting at the merge gate where trust currently evaporates; and an <b>integration observability proxy</b> that watches the brittle wiring between SaaS systems that nobody else monitors. Chapter 10 ranks twelve such opportunities by evidence strength, openness of the field, and realism of the distribution model.'),
    ],
}

CH2 = {
    'num': '2', 'title': 'Methodology: Five Parallel Research Tracks', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'The research was structured as five independent tracks, each assigned to a separate research agent so that no single perspective would dominate the findings. Track R1 examined developer tooling: environments, testing, observability, documentation, code review, and dependency management. Track R2 examined the plumbing layer: B2B integration, the small-business data layer, migration and exit tooling, identity and permissions, legacy modernization, and batch-file interchange. Track R3 hunted specifically for "missing links" — pairs of systems that both exist and both work but were never connected. Track R4 analyzed badly-built categories: software used for ten to twenty years that nearly everyone hates, and the structural reasons it stays bad. Track R5 covered the two frontiers: missing infrastructure for the AI application era, and unsexy industries still running on spreadsheets.'),
        ('p', 'Each track applied the same verification rule, which is the methodological core of this report: <b>every candidate gap was checked against the strongest incumbent solution before being accepted.</b> Docker and Nix exist, so "dev environments are unsolved" was rejected and narrowed to the residual slice that remains — composition, secrets, seed data, and verification. The API contract-testing ecosystem is mature and growing (a reported $2.8B services market), so it was discarded as a gap. LLM tracing was discarded because nine or more mature tools already compete. This screening is what separates the findings from a generic list of startup ideas: everything in this report survived a deliberate attempt to kill it.'),
        ('p', 'Evidence standards required that every claimed pain point trace to a real, dated source — a survey, a vendor report, an engineering post, or a practitioner discussion — seen directly in search results during the research window. No statistics were invented or extrapolated. The evidence window prioritizes 2024-2026, with older statistics included only where explicitly flagged. Two limitations deserve honesty: shared search-quota rate limits forced some tracks to lean on cached result sets rather than fresh verification searches, and a handful of unsexy-industry verticals (field services, small-manufacturing MES, elder care, government legacy) are flagged in Chapter 8 as recommended follow-up research rather than asserted as validated gaps.'),
    ],
}

