        ('p', 'The gaps are not abstract. Practitioners quantify them constantly, in surveys and incident reports that the industry then fails to productize. The chart below collects six of the most telling quantifications found during the research — a trust gap inside the merge gate, a data-entry gap that hollows out the system of record, and an estimating practice that keeps an entire trade vertical in spreadsheet land. Each number corresponds to a validated gap later in the report, and each one is a survey result, not an estimate.'),
        ('chart', '/home/z/my-project/scripts/assets/chart1_pain.png',
         'Figure 1. Quantified pain points across the validated gaps (share of surveyed practitioners; sources as labeled)'),
        ('p', 'Reading the six numbers together reveals the shape of the opportunity. The two SonarSource figures form a single behavioral scandal: almost nobody trusts machine-written code, and barely half of the profession verifies it — which is precisely the gap a verification layer can monetize. The 79% CRM figure means the "system of record" is structurally blind to most of the reality it is supposed to record. And the 85% Excel figure in construction is not a laggard statistic; it is a market the software industry has priced itself out of for thirty years. Where pain is this quantified and this persistent, the absence of a good solution is not an accident — it has structural causes, which Chapter 9 dissects.'),
        ('p', 'The gaps are also countable. Each research lens was forced to defend its candidates against the strongest incumbent before accepting them, and what survived that screening is a measurable body of work: five to eight validated gap deep-dives per lens, thirty-two in total across the five tracks, distilled into the twelve ranked opportunities of Chapter 10. Figure 2 shows how the survivors distribute across the lenses — and why the plumbing and missing-links lenses, which ask what is broken underneath and between working systems, carry the deepest concentrations of unbuilt software.'),
