CH4 = {
    'num': '4', 'title': 'Developer Tooling: Seven Validated Gaps', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'Developer tooling is the most crowded software market on earth, which is exactly why the surviving gaps here are interesting: they persisted through two decades of intense competition. Each of the seven gaps below was checked against its incumbent (Docker and Nix for environments, Playwright retries and BuildPulse for flakiness, Datadog and Grafana for observability, Confluence and Notion for documentation, GitHub review flows, Dependabot and Renovate for dependencies). Each incumbent solves an adjacent problem, and the unsolved remainder is the gap.'),
        ('h2', '4.1  Flaky tests: detection exists, root-cause repair does not'),
        ('p', 'Every serious tool can detect and retry a flaky test; almost nothing on the market can say why it flakes, let alone fix it. A practitioner writing in April 2026 reported roughly 4% of a 1,200-test E2E suite failing spuriously per run, with every failure triggering "a re-run, a triage, a Slack thread" (dev.to, Apr 2026). The industry default fix is the retry loop, described bluntly as a band-aid (Medium, May 2026), and reproducing a flake still requires disabling retries and hand-tuning (charpeni.com, Mar 2025). The gap is newly urgent because LLM-application evals are non-deterministic by construction — "part of your code now returns a different answer every time you run it" (kig.re, Jun 2026) — so every team adopting AI is newly exposed to flake-class CI pain (BuildPulse, Jul 2026). Causal attribution across CI telemetry, traces, and timing distributions is now technically feasible; the product that clusters flake signatures, replays the failing case under instrumentation, and files a diagnosis PR is unbuilt.'),
        ('h2', '4.2  Observability is priced for enterprises, not for the 2-20 person team'),
        ('p', 'Ask what monitoring a small team should use and the honest answer is either a free tier that ends abruptly or a Datadog bill that causes "bill shock" — the phrase is SigNoz marketing copy (Oct 2025), and an entire competitor genre exists to answer "is Datadog worth it?" (uptrace.dev, Jan 2025). More than thirty alternatives were catalogued in a single 2026 roundup with no default winner (hyperping, Jul 2026), which signals an unsolved category rather than a solved one. The missing product is not another backend: it is turnkey, flat-priced, sane-default observability for applications with fewer than ten services, where cost tracks team size instead of cardinality accidents. With OTel standardized, eBPF auto-instrumentation mature, and object storage making retention nearly free, the ingredients for a "you will never get a surprise bill" appliance finally exist.'),
        ('h2', '4.3  Living documentation: nothing goes red'),
        ('p', 'Documentation has no equivalent of a failing test. "Documentation goes stale silently. Unlike code, there is no test suite that goes red when your runbook references a deprecated service" (Atlassian Community, Feb 2026) — and the cost is measurable: new hires take two to three months longer to reach productivity when documentation is not current (GetDX 2025 study, via dosu.dev, Mar 2026). Freshness badges show age, not incorrectness; 2025-era "ask your docs" RAG bots make rot worse by confidently answering from stale pages. The gap is the missing checker: a tool that resolves every entity in a document — service names, endpoints, environment variables, runbook links — against the live repository and cluster, and posts a red mark on the pull request when a document references something deleted or renamed. LLM-based claim extraction just made the cross-referencing step cheap for the first time.'),
        ('h2', '4.4  Code review does not scale to the AI-code era'),
        ('p', 'Review was already the bottleneck before AI multiplied the volume: developers spend about six hours per week on review, roughly 15% of a working week (Microsoft Research, via actual.ai, Mar 2026), and the median engineer at a large company takes thirteen hours to merge a pull request (State of Code Review 2024, via graphite.com). Automated review bots demonstrably slow merges down — average PR closure duration increased from 5h52m to 8h20m after adoption (arXiv, Dec 2024) — while trust in AI output fell from 40% to 29% in a single year (Stack Overflow 2025 Developer Survey, via CIO, Aug 2026). The unsolved part is intent-level review: whether a change does what it claims, is safe to ship, and breaks invariants nobody wrote down. The buildable artifact is a machine-generated "verification dossier" attached to each PR — what the diff claims versus what the tests actually prove — so the human spends their fifteen percent on judgment rather than comprehension. Figure 3 collects the three measurements that define the bottleneck.'),
        ('chart', '/home/z/my-project/scripts/assets/chart6_review.png',
         'Figure 3. The review bottleneck: time invested, time to merge, and what automated review bots did to closure duration (sources as labeled)'),
        ('h2', '4.5  Dependency maintenance: updates are automated, judgment is not'),
        ('p', 'Renovate and Dependabot can open fifty update pull requests a week, but deciding which are safe remains manual, and the blast radius is now worm-scale. The Shai-Hulud npm worm compromised over 500 packages in September 2025 (StepSecurity, Sep 2025; CISA advisory, Sep 2025); a second wave poisoned more than 800 packages and over 25,000 GitHub repositories in December 2025 (backslash.security, Dec 2025); and an ACM study found that 2.30% of dependency updates carry behavioral, invisible breaking changes (dl.acm.org, Jul 2024). Nothing on the market evaluates an upgrade\'s behavioral risk — whether the API changed semantics despite a version bump, whether the new version phones home — before merge. Sandboxed execution is now a commodity, so a CI gate that installs both versions in isolation, diffs exported APIs, network calls, and install scripts, and posts a human-readable verdict is newly buildable.'),
        ('h2', '4.6  Dev environments: containers exist, verified onboarding does not'),
        ('p', 'Every team has a containerfile or a flake, yet "clone to running in ten minutes" still does not exist as a product. The unsolved slice is composition: toolchain, services, secrets, and seed data wired together and verified as one unit, with a self-test that fails with the exact fix command. The pain is diffuse but large — the same GetDX 2025 study that quantified documentation rot attributes two to three months of delayed productivity to onboarding friction, and the record adoption of LocalStack as a cloud-emulation workaround (AWS itself recommends it to cut dev costs, Oct 2025) is evidence that the underlying problem persists. No tool currently treats the developer environment as something with a test suite; an "env doctor" that CI runs on every pull request is the missing shape.'),
        ('h2', '4.7  Test data: a third of engineering time spent fabricating data by hand'),
        ('p', 'Teams spend 30-40% of their time on data preparation for testing (getautonoma.com), production data is restricted and masking is slow (platformengineering.org, Dec 2025), and the state of the art in 2026 is still "a seed script or a Faker-based fixture" (seedfa.st, Apr 2026). Synthetic-data vendors (Tonic, synthesized, mostly.ai) sell almost exclusively to regulated enterprises at six-figure prices, leaving small teams unserved. What is missing is the one-command version: point the tool at a Prisma or SQLAlchemy schema and receive a versioned, referentially-consistent ten-thousand-row dataset with generated edge cases, refreshed on every pull request. Schema-aware LLM generation makes the per-schema modeling cost — historically the reason this never productized — collapse to near zero.'),
        ('p', 'Two candidate gaps from this track were screened out and are worth recording as solved: static analysis and formatting (settled by linters, formatters, and LSP), and feature flags (a crowded space with several dominant platforms). CI itself generates complaints but has clear winners; it is friction, not vacuum.'),
    ],
}

CH5 = {
    'num': '5', 'title': 'The Broken Plumbing: Integration, Data and Exit', 'appendix': False,
    'blocks': [
        ('p', 'The second lens looks underneath working systems. The consistent finding is that connectivity is no longer the problem — APIs, connectors, and iPaaS platforms are everywhere. What is missing is ownership: nobody is paid to notice when the connection between two systems breaks, drifts, or silently loses data. Track R2 synthesized eight validated gaps in this layer, and they share one failure mode: every layer of the modern stack presumes an operator — a data engineer, a platform team, an IT department — that companies below roughly one hundred employees simply do not have. The price of that presumption is not abstract: four independent cost studies put it between $58 for a single expense report and $670,000 added to a healthcare breach, as Figure 4 shows.'),
        ('chart', '/home/z/my-project/scripts/assets/chart5_costs.png',
         'Figure 4. What unowned plumbing costs, in four independent measurements (thousand USD; sources as labeled)'),
        ('h2', '5.1  Integration debt: unowned wiring that breaks silently'),
        ('p', 'Companies run on point-to-point integrations built under deadline pressure, and the accumulated "integration debt" breaks silently at two in the morning (Bluepes, Apr 2025; salable.app, Feb 2026). Zapier and Make meter by the task, which punishes volume; Workato-class platforms start above $50,000 and assume an expert operator; unified APIs collapse to the lowest common denominator. None of them watch the pipeline after setup — no drift alerts, no replay, no failure ownership. The pressure is compounding: task-specific AI agents are projected to integrate with 40% of enterprise applications by the end of 2026, up from under 5% in 2025 (Gabe Veach, LinkedIn, Apr 2026), which multiplies the number of brittle pairwise links exactly when no one is watching them. Figure 5 sets that incoming wave of agent wiring against the CRM blindness it compounds: an eightfold rise in integrated applications, landing on a system of record that already loses 79% of its opportunity data.'),
        ('chart', '/home/z/my-project/scripts/assets/chart4_agents.png',
         'Figure 5. The unwatched wiring wave: projected agent integration share 2025-2026 (left) and the CRM data blindness it lands on (right)'),
        ('h2', '5.2  The SMB data layer: the modern data stack ends at employee ~100'),
        ('p', 'The Fivetran-Snowflake-dbt-BI stack presumes an analytics engineer; below that threshold, companies revert to CSV email attachments and "ductaped spreadsheets." Seventy data leaders and practitioners agree the current data stack is too complex (Medium, Mar 2025), and 63% of practitioners spend more than 20% of their time simply coping with data-stack complexity (The Modern Data Company, Aug 2025). The 2025-26 wave of "AI analysts" inherits exactly this fragmented connector landscape and its stale, permission-wrong data. The open slot is an opinionated, fully managed data layer for one vertical — normalize Shopify, ads, ERP, and shipping into one owned schema, exposed as both metrics and an API that the company\'s agents can query.'),
        ('h2', '5.3  Exit and migration tooling: leaving is archaeology'),
        ('callout', '$315K', 'average vendor lock-in cost per enterprise migration; 62% of organizations cite data migration as the ERP failure driver (Kong, Jun 2026; companieshistory.com, Jun 2026)'),
        ('p', 'Regulators are killing egress fees — the EU Data Act\'s portability obligations became applicable in September 2025 — but the mechanics of leaving remain hand-built: destination vendors build generous, shallow import wizards, and nothing exports workflow logic, the two hundred saved automations and permission model that constitute the real lock-in. The extreme case is ERP, where mid-size projects average $7.1 million and 17.4 months with a 68% failure rate (Panorama 2025, via kreativecoretech.com), and where 62% of organizations name data migration as the failure driver. Even trivial exits are scrambles: Notion Mail\'s 2026 sunset gave users weeks to export while connected syncs broke (TechCrunch, Jun 2026). Migration-as-a-service for one fleeing path — a dying platform to its rival, data plus workflow translation at a fixed fee — has no dedicated vendor, and regulation is now creating the demand.'),
        ('h2', '5.4  Identity syncs accounts, not permissions'),
        ('p', 'SCIM provisions login accounts, but each application\'s internal permission graph drifts silently — offboarding removes the login, not the access. The capability is widespread but gated: most SaaS applications support SCIM "but you can\'t [use it] without an enterprise plan" (stitchflow.com, Jan 2026), and where it is absent, IT teams hand-roll workarounds (ssotax.org). The enterprise answer, SailPoint-class identity governance, costs six figures and assumes an IT department. Nothing reads the role-and-permission graph inside each application — many have no API for it at all. Browser automation plus LLMs newly make a read-only "permission census" scanner buildable: crawl each admin console, diff permission state over time, alert on orphaned access, and emit the audit table that SOC 2 reviewers keep asking for.'),
        ('h2', '5.5  Legacy modernization: the crisis is knowledge, not code'),
        ('p', 'Between 220 and 800 billion lines of COBOL still settle roughly 80% of the world\'s financial transactions, maintained by a generation now walking toward retirement (phasechange.ai, Oct 2025). AI code translation attracts the headlines — a single blog post about AI COBOL translation briefly wiped some $30 billion off IBM\'s market capitalization (devops.com, Feb 2026) — but "translating code isn\'t the same as modernizing," because the missing artifact is the recovered business rule, not the syntax. The long tail is mid-market: AS/400, Delphi, and Access line-of-business applications with no tooling and no staff to validate a rewrite. The sellable object is the executable behavior spec — a characterization test suite extracted by interviewing domain experts — sold independently of any rewrite.'),
        ('h2', '5.6  The spreadsheet is the world\'s actual data layer'),
        ('p', 'Eighty-five percent of construction professionals still use Excel for estimating and costing (premiercs.com, 2026); unmanaged patient data in spreadsheets adds an average of $670,000 to healthcare breach costs (blueBriX, 2026); and inter-company exchange still runs on nightly SFTP drops and EDI batches with per-client custom scripts (filefeed.io, Feb 2026). Two decades of "Excel killers" failed because they demanded greenfield rebuilds that lose fifteen years of formulas and macros — so users keep shadow copies and the "system" bifurcates. The missing product absorbs the workbook instead of replacing it: point it at the operational spreadsheet, auto-generate the schema, change-tracking, audit log, and API around the same file, and let humans keep Excel as the interface. LLMs can finally read a gnarly workbook — formulas, structure, VBA — and infer the schema underneath.'),
        ('h2', '5.7  Real-time plumbing is enterprise-only by construction'),
        ('p', 'Kafka-class streaming is architecturally correct but operationally absurd for small teams — "overkill for simple task queues" (inteca.com, May 2025) — and teams flee it over broker cost, storage growth, and rebalancing delays (automq.com, Jun 2026). A whole 2025-26 product category exists just to simplify it. Small firms do not need a platform; they need the outcome: a record changed, downstream applications see it now, idempotently, with retries. Durable-execution primitives and queue-as-a-service finally make "webhooks with guarantees" — exact-once fan-out, replay, a dead-letter dashboard, schema versioning — the shippable 10% of Kafka that 90% of small integrators actually need.'),
        ('h2', '5.8  Inter-company integration still runs on batch files'),
        ('p', 'B2B SaaS vendors hand-roll per-client file ingestion with no tooling: monitored SFTP folders, bespoke CSV and XLSX parsers, and reconciliation over email. Enterprise EDI gateways are priced for the Fortune 500, and iPaaS connectors target APIs rather than the arbitrary files clients actually send. The per-client custom-script tax hides inside professional-services line items, which is precisely why no standalone product ever formed. Arbitrary-file schema inference is newly cheap, so "drop-box to database" — monitored inbox in, auto-detected schema, validated rows out via API — sold per client per month to the SaaS vendor rather than the end user, is now a product where before it was a services engagement.'),
        ('p', 'The synthesis of this chapter is one sentence: <b>connectivity exists; ownership does not.</b> Entry is funded everywhere (generous import wizards, free tiers, onboarding teams) while exit and reliability are funded nowhere — and the agent wave is about to inherit exactly this unmaintained wiring.'),
    ],
}
